asr-009370
08:45:01.000 --> 08:45:16.160
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: and I will show you your priorities. The proposed budget is 7.2 trillion pesos. But the 7.2 trillion pesos headline does not tell the whole story.

asr-009371
08:45:16.380 --> 08:45:37.540
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: Government expects approximately 5.2 trillion in revenues in 2027. Interest payments alone will reach approximately 1.114 trillion. Principal amortization is about 1.59 trillion.

asr-009372
08:45:39.220 --> 08:45:46.360
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: Perhaps payments are financing transactions and are, strictly speaking,

asr-009373
08:45:46.380 --> 08:45:55.400
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: not part of the 7.2 trillion expenditure program, but they remain actual obligations of the Treasury.

asr-009374
08:45:56.820 --> 08:46:12.520
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: Taken together, that service reaches approximately 2.7 trillion, equivalent to almost 52 for every 100 pesos of projected government revenue.

asr-009375
08:46:13.520 --> 08:46:15.080
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: 52 pesos.

asr-009376
08:46:16.380 --> 08:46:30.140
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: Then, government must provide for the national tax allotment, salaries, pensions, statutory contributions, and other mandatory expenditures.

asr-009377
08:46:30.780 --> 08:46:43.740
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: So while we speak of a 7.2 trillion budget, the amount over which government actually has room to make choices is much smaller.

asr-009378
08:46:44.780 --> 08:46:46.140
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: The amount of 2.6 trillion.

asr-009379
08:46:46.380 --> 08:46:52.420
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: About 36 percent of the 7.2 trillion budget.

asr-009380
08:46:53.700 --> 08:47:08.080
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: Kung ganoon, pagkatapos bayaran ang utang at ang mga obligasyon, hindi na maaring pagpaliban, mas maliit ang perang tunay nating mapapagpasyahan.

asr-009381
08:47:09.200 --> 08:47:12.680
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: That is fiscal reality behind this budget.

asr-009382
08:47:12.680 --> 08:47:19.060
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: This budget will also operate in a difficult economic environment.

asr-009383
08:47:20.700 --> 08:47:32.120
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: GDP grew by only 2.3 percent in the second quarter of 2026, after growth of 2.8 percent in the first quarter.

asr-009384
08:47:32.640 --> 08:47:42.120
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: Industry contracted by 2.4 percent, while gross capital formation fell by 9.2%.

asr-009385
08:47:42.680 --> 08:47:51.360
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: At the same time, inflation remained high at 6.1 percent in August.

asr-009386
08:47:52.740 --> 08:48:02.500
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: For the bottom 30 percent of Filipino households, inflation was even higher at 8.2 percent.

asr-009387
08:48:03.300 --> 08:48:11.740
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: These numbers matter because slower growth limits income and employment opportunities.

asr-009388
08:48:12.480 --> 08:48:13.100
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: While

asr-009399
08:48:13.100 --> 08:48:17.360
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: Inflation reduces what those incomes can buy.

asr-009400
08:48:18.580 --> 08:48:35.020
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: Para sa ordinaryong pamilya, simply lang ang ibig sabihin nito, mas mabagal na paglago ng kita, pero mas mabilis ang pagtaas ng presyo ng mga bilihin at serbisyo.

asr-009401
08:48:35.900 --> 08:48:42.500
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: This is why the quality of government spending matters as much as its size.

asr-009402
08:48:42.680 --> 08:49:07.760
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: The minority recognizes the need for fiscal consolidation. Under the government's medium-term framework, the deficit is expected to decline from approximately 5.4% of GDP in 2026 to 3.5% by 2030.

asr-009403
08:49:07.760 --> 08:49:24.760
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: But the revenues remain broadly around 15-16% of GDP, while disbursements decline from approximately 21.2% to 19.1%.

asr-009404
08:49:26.080 --> 08:49:31.280
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: Much of the adjustments, therefore, comes from expenditure restraint.

asr-009405
08:49:31.280 --> 08:49:36.160
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: There is nothing wrong with spending discipline.

asr-009406
08:49:36.160 --> 08:49:44.980
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: Government shall eliminate waste, duplication, and projects that cannot be implemented.

asr-009407
08:49:46.400 --> 08:49:53.260
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: But restraint should protect essential investments in people and productive capacity.

asr-009408
08:49:54.600 --> 08:49:58.780
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: Education illustrates the pressure on our fiscal space.

asr-009409
08:49:58.780 --> 08:50:05.780
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: The proposed DepEd budget is approximately P976 billion.

asr-009410
08:50:06.160 --> 08:50:13.200
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: While interest payments alone are approximately P1.114 trillion.

asr-009411
08:50:14.260 --> 08:50:22.980
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: We therefore spend about P138 billion more on interest than on the entire Department of Education.

asr-009412
08:50:25.100 --> 08:50:33.180
Minority Leader Marcelino C. Libanan: Education sector spending is approximately 3.99% of GDP,

asr-009413
08:50:33.180 --> 08:50:35.180
